! Negation is sensitive to event structure

* 不 (bù) is a frequent negator of habits, general facts, willingness, and future predictions.
* 没 (méi; 没有 méiyǒu) often denies that an event occurred, and negates existential/possessive 有 (yǒu).

Compare:
* 我不吃饭。 — Wǒ bù chī fàn. — I don't eat / won't eat (depending on context).
* 我没吃饭。 — Wǒ méi chī fàn. — I haven't eaten / didn't eat.
* 我没有书。 — Wǒ méiyǒu shū. — I don't have a book.

''Avoid the shortcut'' “不 = present, 没 = past.” It is inaccurate. This distinction interacts with aspect, modality, and pragmatics.

